---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Artwork from '../components/Artwork.astro';
import { url } from '../utils';

const pictured = await getEntry('works', 'college-farm-1-richmond-lowlands');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Greg Hansell, an Australian painter in handmade earth pastels and oil, based at Windsor in the Hawkesbury region of New South Wales."
>
  <div class="container section about">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About Greg Hansell</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Greg Hansell is an Australian painter in the traditionalist, realist school, known for
        earth pastels he makes himself from crushed rocks and clays.
      </p>
      <p>
        Born in Goulburn, New South Wales, in 1949, Greg studied art at St George Technical
        College in the late 1970s, has painted full time since 1980, and made study tours of
        Europe in 1982 and 1985. He credits the Renaissance masters as the inspiration for most
        of his work.
      </p>
      <p>
        He lives on the historic Peninsula at Windsor, in a house with a studio overlooking the
        Hawkesbury River and a large garden that often finds its way into his work. He has
        lived in the Hawkesbury for more than 30 years, drawn in part by its association with
        Arthur Streeton and Charles Conder, who painted there in the heyday of Australian
        landscape painting.
      </p>

      <h2>The work</h2>
      <p>
        Greg crushes rocks and clays into pigment and, with no added binder, makes earth pastels
        of the highest colour permanence. He also works with Schmincke pastels and paints in oil.
      </p>
      <p>
        His subjects are close views of the Australian landscape and its history: the shadows and
        textures of weathered surfaces, historic houses and the Victorian clutter of their
        interiors, farm sheds and country roads. He is well known for his corrugated iron series
        and for artworks with witty, often political titles.
      </p>

      <h2>Teaching</h2>
      <p>
        Greg has taught at the Royal Art Society of New South Wales since 1997 and is its Art
        School Director, as well as a Fellow and Council member. From 2011 he was artist in
        residence at the Australiana Pioneer Village in Wilberforce.
      </p>

      <h2>Recognition</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Finalist in the Archibald Prize, and hung in the Wynne Prize at least 19 times, at the Art Gallery of New South Wales.</li>
        <li>Finalist in the Dobell Drawing Prize, 2005.</li>
        <li>Twice winner of the Mosman Art Prize.</li>
        <li>Centenary of Federation Medal, 2001, for services to community arts and Windsor Library.</li>
        <li>Three exhibitions commissioned by Historic Houses Trust Members at The Mint, Sydney, in 2006, 2008 and 2012.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><a class="more-link" href={url('/exhibitions/')}>Exhibitions and prizes</a></p>

      <h2>Collections</h2>
      <p>
        Greg's work is held in public and corporate collections including the Historic Houses
        Trust of NSW, Hawkesbury City Council, Camden Council, the Commonwealth Bank and ANZ.
      </p>

      <h2>The book</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={url('/book/')}><cite>Greg Hansell: Purity of Australian Light</cite></a>, by Lou
        Klepac (The Beagle Press, 2024), covers his work from 1982 to the present.
      </p>
    </article>

    {pictured?.data.image && (
      <figure class="about__art">
        <Artwork image={pictured.data.image} alt={pictured.data.imageAlt ?? pictured.data.title}
          sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 30vw, 100vw" />
        <figcaption class="meta"><a href={url(`/works/${pictured.id}/`)}><cite>{pictured.data.title}</cite></a>, {pictured.data.medium?.toLowerCase()}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about { display: grid; gap: var(--space-6); align-items: start; }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .about { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 38rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .about__art { position: sticky; top: var(--space-5); }
  }
  .about__art { margin: 0; }
  .about__art figcaption { margin-top: var(--space-3); }
  .prose h2 { font-size: 1.75rem; }
</style>
